docs/plan/EXCLUDE_CASE_MATCHING.md
1 # Exclude case matching: `--match-case` default, `--ignore-case`, and the one-line prompt
2 
3 **Status**: Active — owner: "yes" (2026-11-20, D-2026-11-20-1), after the interview begun 2026-10-21 and resumed 2026-11-20 settled Q1 to Q11; Design §Matching revised the same day (D-2026-11-20-4): `--ignore-case` widens the pattern and leaves names untouched
4 **Created**: 2026-10-21
5 **Supersedes**: `docs/plan/MIRROR_GLOB_DEST_ONLY.md` (Draft r3, same day — its mechanism options are all withdrawn; its prior-art measurements of robocopy and rsync stand and are referenced below); the first bullet of `docs/RELEASE_NOTES_1_0.md` "Known limitations shipped open", once this ships
6 **Decision ref**: D-2026-11-20-1
7 
8 ## Goal
9 
10 A user who gives a pattern to a command that can delete is told, in one line and in their own words, how that pattern will match on this run, and chooses. Patterns match exactly as typed by default (`--match-case`, the rsync rule, the same on every machine). `--ignore-case` makes every pattern in the command ignore capital letters, on the source walk and in the destination's deletion scope alike, on every machine. When a delete option is in effect, `-y` is absent, and the user has NOT chosen a mode (neither `--match-case` nor `--ignore-case` typed, Q9), the existing confirmation prompt gains one line that echoes the user's actual pattern and the spellings it will and will not match; with either option typed the user has already chosen, so only the normal delete warning prints. `-y` bypasses the prompt as today. `copy`, which has no prompt and gains none, prints the same line as a note and goes on (Q3). Nothing depends on which filesystem the destination has.
11 
12 ## Owner requirements (2026-10-21, verbatim where quoted)
13 
14 - "FAST, SIMPLE, RELIABLE" — the earlier proposals (disk-detected case mode; regex-style `(?i)` prefix; Unicode-table pairing; filesystem-lookup pairing) were rejected as violating two of the three.
15 - "it cannot be internally inconsistent" — one reading of a pattern per run, the same for what is copied and what is deleted.
16 - "I would expect ß to mean that exact character, not transliterated to ss" — no fold or transliteration changes which letters a pattern names. Ignoring case maps one letter to the same letter in another case, never to a different letter or a different number of letters.
17 - "same as the decades old established tools, unless you have a compelling, real-world reason to subvert people's expectations" — rsync matches exactly; robocopy matches with Windows' own case-insensitive rule; both protect excluded destination-only entries from deletion and both delete a destination-only entry no exclude names (measured and read from source in `MIRROR_GLOB_DEST_ONLY.md` §Prior art).
18 - "it's a flag users will only look for after they've lost data" — hence the prompt line: the choice is presented at runtime, before the delete, not discovered afterwards.
19 - "when data loss is possible, this cannot be ambiguous even to novices" — the prompt line says what blit will do, in the user's own pattern, not what a filesystem might do.
20 - "we have a prompt whenever a delete option is specified without -y, so if -y is not included and a CLI option with a pattern is included, there should be a short, 1 line blurb about the matching issue … with -y bypassing it. --match-case is the default, and --ignore-case does extra work on case sensitive filesystems."
21 - "that requires us to know what the remote target's filesystem is, so that's a daemon change" — the line therefore states blit's own rule, which is identical everywhere, and needs no probe of the destination's disk.
22 - "the example needs to be the actual string the user entered."
23 - "if the user specifies --ignore-case and not -y then the message should not print, just the normal delete warning" — the line exists to present a choice not yet made; once made, it is silent.
24 - "if we have an include from, we need an exclude from. so --files-from needs an exclusion analog. every entry in either needs to be exact or a [pattern], so users with a 50 line file won't miss something and delete all their .Jpg files" — hence `--exclude-from FILE` and `--include-from FILE` (one pattern per line, blank lines and `#` comments ignored; the line rule is the one `--files-from` already follows, Q5 — not rsync's, which keeps edge whitespace), each line treated exactly as one more command-line pattern under the run's one case mode; `--files-from` stays the literal name list it is today. (Owner wrote "regex"; blit's patterns are globs — `*.jpg` — and a file line is an exact name or a glob, the same syntax as the option. Reading confirmed by the owner 2026-11-20, Q7 "a": globs here, regular expressions in a plan of their own.)
25 
26 - 2026-11-20, on the measurements recorded below and in Open questions: `--ignore-case` covers patterns only, never the `--files-from` list (Q2 "a"); a pattern file is read by the rule `--files-from` already follows, both ends stripped (Q5 "a", after "these are extremely consequential decisions that I cannot simply answer in a vacuum" and the measurements that followed); a saved job keeps the patterns read, not the file's path (Q6 "a"); `copy` prints the matching line as a note with no question (Q3 "b", after `copy` was measured overwriting a destination file an exclude missed by case); a typed `--match-case` silences the line exactly as `--ignore-case` does (Q9 "a"); the three 2026-10-19 cases are expected behavior, not a defect (Q4 "a", after rsync was run deleting the same entries); `?` and classes keep counting bytes, and a class holding a non-ASCII character is refused (Q10 "b"); patterns here are globs, and regular expressions — "why can't we support regex in both?" — get a plan of their own (Q7 "a"); a pattern-file line written as an rsync rule (`- `, `+ `, `!`) refuses the run (Q8 "a").
27 - 2026-11-20, on the other verbs that take patterns: "find should work identically to the unix find command. I don't know what check is." — `find` therefore leaves this plan for one of its own (Q11); `check`, once explained, takes the transfer verbs' pattern options (Q11 "a").
28 - 2026-11-20: "yes" — the plan is Active (D-2026-11-20-1).
29 
30 ## Prior art: how established tools read a pattern file (owner question 2026-11-20: "do you need to see the rsync source? how does robocopy do it?")
